          "We examined 15 columns across 1 data file.",
          "#### Careless Responding",
          "2 distinct respondents were flagged for **straightlining**: giving the same answer for at least 80% of the items in a multi-item scale of 5 items or more. One respondent can be flagged in several scales; the table below is **one row per person**, and the *Longest run* column gives the run that triggered the strongest flag, so you can check it against the raw data.",
          "Of these, **1 were flagged *only* by short-scale straightlining** (a run of identical answers on a scale of 7 items or fewer). On a short, one-directional scale, answering consistently is often normal, coherent responding rather than carelessness — treat these as weak signals and inspect the actual responses before excluding anyone.",
          "These are prompts to inspect those rows, not definitive judgements.",
          "\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Respondent = c(\"41\", \"5\"), \"Blocks flagged\" = 2:1, \n    Scales = c(\"panas (1-5, 8 items); rse (1-5, 6 items)\", \"rse (1-5, 6 items)\"\n    ), \"Longest run\" = c(\"same answer 8 times in a row, out of 8 items (flagged at 7)\", \n    \"same answer 6 times in a row, out of 6 items (flagged at 5)\"\n    ), \"Short-scale only\" = c(\"no\", \"yes\")), row.names = 1:2, class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 20, FALSE)\n```\n",
          "**What this does not cover.** Even in a screened file, these checks only look at multi-item rating scales — the only place where a repeated or flat answer pattern is interpretable. Single questions, open text, demographics and any item outside a detected scale block are never examined. A respondent can therefore answer most of a survey carelessly and still not be flagged here, and finding nothing is not evidence that a dataset is free of careless responding."
        ]
